AR composition: the UNMODIFIED Menagerie panda.xml on a simple table whose top
sits at z = 0 (the robot base plane), extending down 0.73 m to the operator's
physical floor. No skybox, no ground plane: the background is passthrough, and
cpp/scene_renderer.cpp skips mjGEOM_PLANE outright.

THIS FILE IS THE ONLY TRACKED FILE IN THIS DIRECTORY. Everything beside it --
panda.xml, assets/, LICENSE -- is fetched by scripts/fetch-menagerie.sh at a
pinned commit and is gitignored. That is also why the include below has no path
in it: MuJoCo resolves an INCLUDED file's `meshdir` against the included file's
own directory and DROPS the `meshdir` when the wrapper lives elsewhere
(measured on mujoco 3.11.0), so the wrapper must be a sibling of panda.xml.
See the long note at the top of scripts/fetch-menagerie.sh.

The 0.73 is cpp/frames.hpp's kTransMjFromXr[2] and is shared by every scene.
What makes one calibration legal for all of them is that each wrapper puts its
table TOP at z = 0 with the robot base on it. The tables are otherwise sized to
their own arm -- this one is 0.45 x 0.4 at x = 0.15 for a ~0.85 m reach, the
SO-101's is smaller -- but the z = 0 plane is the load-bearing part and is
common. A wrapper that floats its robot above the table, or thickens the table
downward from z = 0, silently invalidates the 0.73 for that scene only.

NO <keyframe> HERE, and that is not an omission: panda.xml:281 already authors
one named `home`, with a matching `ctrl=` (qpos-only would make the A-reset
drop every actuator command to zero and the arm collapse). The SO-101 wrapper
has to author its own because so101.xml ships none.
-->
<mujoco model="panda AR scene">
  <include file="panda.xml"/>

  <worldbody>
    <geom name="table" type="box" size="0.45 0.4 0.365" pos="0.15 0 -0.365"
        rgba="0.46 0.36 0.25 1"/>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>
